<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../Graphics/Australia_20050921.jpg"><img class="PicBorder" src="Graphics/AustraliaSmall_20050921.jpg" align="right"></a>
<u>09-SEP-2005 10:15 PM (AUS Time)</u><br>
The flight to Sydney was pretty uneventful but meeting up with Ching was something I'll never forget.  Ching was my MeetIn Sydney sponser.  She was kind enough to help me out and offer M and I a place to stay right in the center of downtown Sydney.  All of our communication had been via email including how we were going to meet up and it had never occurred to me that I should try to contact her cell phone number before I got into Sydney.  This was going to get me later on.
<p>
As we arrived into Sydney, we knew to get a Weekly Pass that allows you to get on any train/bus/ferry and it was still a better deal that getting daily passes, however it took us some time to figure out and the lady at the train desk was not being to helpful.  Almost kinda insulting.  I'm sure she was just tired hearing the same question over and over again. Which pass do I get for this or that.  I dismissed the whole thing but really if you are going to sit there and an international airport known to receive a lot of international tourists, what else would you expect?
<p>
Anyway, after figuring out what to do. We got our pass and caught the train to the pre-selected meeting point called Town Hall.  We had gotten there around 2:30 PM when I attempted to call Ching to let her know we were there.  As I called using my cell phone and a pay phone, it told me I could not get connected to that number.  Now I freaked as that was our only way to communicate and it wasn't working. There was no Plan B.  I had noticed that the phone number was missing one digit. I didn't know which one unfortunately.  I was kicking myself. Why hadn't I tried to test out the number earlier?!?  In a city as large as Sydney how are two people going to find each other. Heck, I didn't even know what she looked liked. However, I had sent her our pictures so she at least knew what we looked like.  
<p>
I had given Ching my cell phone number.  All we could do is sit and hope she would find us or call us. So we went to the food court and relaxed and grabbed some lunch.  No luck after an hour and I decided that I would have to come up with a Plan B. That would be to check into a hostel nearby. I had called one up and made sure they had rooms available.  
<p>
M and I decided to leave and go there as it was starting to get cold and we had all of our luggage to drag around and we didn't want to do it in the dark.  We went back to the train station to get on the appropriate to get off at the Central stop were the hostel was.  Not being able to figure out how to get down to the train, we saw an elevator and decided to take that.  Hitting the down button we waited and just before the doors opened, the most amazing thing happening.  And if you never believed in fate before, you will after reading this.
<p>
There was this girl, Asian, pointing at us.  Now remember I didn't know what Ching looked like but she knew what we looked like.  And in that place, in that time, we met, by sheer luck --- sheer fate.  And I am now a true believer in fate.  It was Ching, M and I were so relieved.  WE BEAT THE IMPOSSIBLE!!!!
<p>
Ching took us back to her place. On the way she explained that another Meeting member from MeetInVancouver had also arrived today and was staying with her so conditions would be a little cramped at her place, but it didn't bother me.  We had discussed what happened and I told her I was missing a phone number digit so I couldn't call her and she told me the number I gave her rang up some other guy.  Later on we found out that one of the digits was transposed but I don't know if I did it or if she did it.  
<p>
When we got to Ching's apartment, I met Robert, the other meetin.org member.  Robert had quit work and been living abroad ever since. He spent time in Asia for a year and would work whereever he was earning money so he can go to a new destination. Robert's pasttime was cooking and from what I smelled, it was damn good.  Robert had been telling about his adventures and run-ins with the authorities.  In China, he went with an American from Oregon of all places :), who was dressed in combat fatigues.  As this American claimed, this is how people dress in Oregon (yeah it's true to some extent but not everyone dressed that way), but really that was just plain stupid if you asked me.  You go around looking for trouble and you'll find it.  Anyway, Robert was with him and the Chinese Army stopped them both and thought he was a spy.  Robert spent 3 days under duress and house arrest before they finally believed he wasn't a spy.  Anyway, Robert was going to live in Sydney now and was looking for a job to support himself.  Upon leaving Sydney, he found a place to stay and had job prospects, so there ya go. Good for you, Robert.
<p>
I had also exchanged my adventures with Robert and Ching before M and I decided to get some dinner.  We headed towards Darling Harbour where the area was once again very lively with restaurants, bars, and clubs.  Here, however, were young and old people.  M and I decided to eat in the Blackbird Cafe which had great veggie choices.  And to our surprise not only was the food extremely tasty but we lucked out to a fireworks show from the Star City building across the harbour.  Noone really knew what the fireworks were for, but it made the night that much more special. M and I fell in love with Sydney right then and there and even after I write this, I have to admit, Sydney was my most favourite city in Australia that we visited.  
<p>
Afterwards, we decided to walk around the harbour and check out an old battleship and submarine at the Maritime museum.  Feeling tired, we headed back to Ching's place for some rest.
